{"toolCallId":"call_2b3b224827754ddfaf09c51b5582320b","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"58:| `ANCHORS_VALID`      | ERROR    | spec docs with anchored templates | Anchors present, paired and not nested (adr-NNN may hold adr-NNN-*); a v2.3 doc also keeps each anchor its template requires |\n59-| `FOLDER_NAMING`      | ERROR    | Folder path   | Folder follows ###-short-name convention       |\n60-| `FRONTMATTER_VALID`  | ERROR    | spec docs     | YAML frontmatter properly structured           |\n61:| `COMPLEXITY_MATCH`   | WARNING  | All levels    | Content metrics match declared level           |\n62-| `AI_PROTOCOLS`       | ERROR    | Level 3/3+    | AI execution protocols present                 |\n63-| `LEVEL_MATCH`        | ERROR    | All files     | Level consistent across all spec files         |\n64:| `SPEC_DOC_SUFFICIENCY` | ERROR  | spec docs, goal.md | Anchor sufficiency; for `goal.md` also the durable budget (one limit: fail past 4000) and binding-row existence |\n65-| `FRONTMATTER_VALUES` | WARNING  | packet docs   | `contextType` or `importance_tier` outside sk-create-frontmatter's `frontmatter-values.json`; aliases are legal |\n66-| `SOURCE_TAGS`        | WARNING  | research/ + review/ | `[SOURCE: path:line]` tags that name a gone file, a moved file or a line past the end, in packets created after the cutoff |\n67-| `MEASURED_BEHAVIOR` | WARNING  | spec.md stamped v2.4+ | `measured-behavior` anchor present with the changed command's verbatim output, or an `N/A:` line with a reason |\n68-\n69-> **Partial reference:** The table above covers the most commonly-encountered rules. The authoritative, complete rule set and their canonical severities live in [`runtime/cli/lib/validator-registry.json`](../../runtime/cli/lib/validator-registry.json).\n70-\n71-### AC_CLOSURE\n72-\n73-`AC_CLOSURE` decides whether a packet may be closed. It is registered at ERROR severity and runs by default; set `SPECKIT_AC_CLOSURE=false` to opt out.\n74-\n75-**Rule ID:** `AC_CLOSURE`\n76-\n77-**Scope:** Levels 2, 3 and 3+. Level 1 keeps acceptance criteria inline in `spec.md` and is never gated.\n78-\n79-**Rollout:** forward-only behind `SPECKIT_AC_CLOSURE_CUTOFF` (default `2026-08-30`), compared against the `Created` date in the packet's `spec.md` metadata table. A packet created on or before the cutoff, or whose date cannot be read, is advisory on **every** branch — presence, waiver integrity and completion alike — and is never blocked. A malformed override falls back to the default rather than being compared as a string, which would otherwise grandfather the whole repository. This mirrors the `CANONICAL_SAVE_CUTOFF` pattern rather than introducing a third grandfathering mechanism.\n80-\n81-**Failure modes:**\n82-\n83-| Condition | Result |\n84-|---|---|\n85-| Post-cutoff packet with no `acceptance-criteria.md` | `fail` — the document is required at these levels |\n86-| A row marked `Waived` or `Superseded` naming no ADR | `fail` — an unbacked waiver is not a pass |\n87-| A criterion row that cannot be parsed, or an unbalanced code fence | `fail` — a dropped row would empty the table and close the packet |\n88-| Duplicate criterion ids | `fail` — the tally is ambiguous |\n89-| A row citing an ADR that `decision-record.md` does not contain | `fail` — the record is the whole justification |\n90-| A packet claiming completion with an `Unmet` row | `fail` — this is the closure gate |\n91-| An `Unmet` row while the packet is still in progress | `info` — work in progress is expected to carry open criteria |\n92-| Pre-cutoff packet | `info` — grandfathered |\n93-\n94-**Waiver contract:** a criterion may only be dropped or replaced through a decision record. The Waiver cell must name `ADR-NNN`, and every ADR it names must be declared in `decision-record.md` as a heading, a bold list item or a table row, outside any code fence. `ADR-1` and `ADR-001` are the same record.\n--\n323:- `**/memory/**` - Generated continuity artifacts use different validation (ANCHORS_VALID)\n324-- `**/templates/**` - Template files are expected to have placeholders\n325-\n326-### Examples\n327-\n328-❌ **Fail:**\n329-```markdown\n330-## Metadata\n331-\n332-| Field | Value |\n333-|-------|-------|\n334-| **Type** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Feature type] |  ← FLAGGED\n335-```\n336-\n337-✅ **Pass:**\n338-```markdown\n339-## Metadata\n340-\n341-| Field | Value |\n342-|-------|-------|\n343-| **Type** | Feature |\n344-```\n345-\n346-### How to Fix\n347-\n348-Replace placeholder text with actual content:\n349-\n350-1. Find the flagged line in the output\n351-2. Replace `[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: description]` with the actual value\n352-3. Remove the entire `[...]` block, not just the inner text\n353-\n--\n400:## 6. ANCHORS_VALID\n401-\n402-**Severity:** ERROR, nesting included  \n403-**Description:** Checks anchors in each spec doc whose template defines them, ignoring fenced code, and reports missing, unpaired, repeated or nested anchors as errors.\n404-\n405-### What Are Anchors?\n406-\n407-Anchors are structured markers that define semantic boundaries in a packet's continuity documents. They enable:\n408-- Extracting one named region without parsing the whole document\n409-- Section-specific context loading\n410-- Template-contract checks that assert a required region is present\n411-\n412-### Anchor Format\n413-\n414-```markdown\n415-<!-- ANCHOR:id -->\n416-Content goes here...\n417-<!-- /ANCHOR:id -->\n418-```\n419-\n420-### Rules\n421-\n422-1. **Scope:** the check reads the level's spec docs whose own template defines anchors. A document whose template ships no anchors is skipped, so an anchored surface is never demanded of a document that never had one. `research/research.md` and `review/review-report.md` are skipped as free-form workflow artifacts, and a phase parent is exempt because its lean document set passes without anchor checks.\n423-2. **Fenced code is ignored.** An anchor written inside a code fence is invisible to the check, which is why the examples below can show raw markup. A marker quoted in inline code is ignored by the nesting check, while the missing, unpaired and repeated checks still count it.\n424-3. **Names are matched exactly.** An id is a run of lower-case letters, digits and hyphens, and the close marker must repeat the open marker's string exactly.\n425-4. **Error findings make the rule fail:** a checked document with no anchors (`no anchors found`), an anchor opened twice (`duplicate anchor '<name>'`), an anchor that is never closed (`is never closed`), a close with no matching open (`is closed but never opened`), and a close count that exceeds the open count (`is closed more times than it is opened`).\n426-5. **Nesting findings are errors:** an anchor opened inside another (`is opened inside '<outer>'`), a close that arrives before its open (`is closed before it is opened`), and a close while another anchor is still open (`is closed while '<outer>' is still open`). A document with nesting findings fails, and they are reported alongside any other error findings.\n427-6. **One nesting is allowed:** an `adr-NNN` anchor may hold `adr-NNN-*` anchors, so `adr-001` can contain `adr-001-context`.\n428-7. **A v2.3 document keeps its required sections.** A document whose `SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE` stamp reads v2.3 or later fails with `required anchor '<id>' is missing` for each anchor its rendered template requires and the document lacks; the finding names the `## <heading>` to restore. Sections under an `L2:`, `L3:`, `L3+:` or `FIX ADDENDUM:` heading are optional, and `decision-record.md` requires none because its ADR sections are named by the author. A document stamped v2.2 or earlier, or unstamped, keeps the integrity-only check.\n429-8. **Template order is not checked.** The rule never compares a document's anchor order against the template's. A template that moves a section would regrade the whole corpus on every commit, which turns the template into a moving target rather than a contract.\n430-\n--\n618:## 9. COMPLEXITY_MATCH\n619-\n620-**Severity:** WARNING\n621-**Description:** Validates that declared complexity level matches actual content metrics (user stories, phases, tasks).\n622-\n623-### Expected Ranges by Level\n624-\n625-| Level | User Stories | Phases  | Tasks     |\n626-| ----- | ------------ | ------- | --------- |\n627-| 1     | 1-2          | 2-3     | 5-15      |\n628-| 2     | 2-4          | 3-5     | 15-50     |\n629-| 3/3+  | 4-15         | 5-12    | 50-200    |\n630-\n631-### Detection Patterns\n632-\n633-| Metric       | Pattern Searched                           |\n634-| ------------ | ------------------------------------------ |\n635-| User Stories | `### User Story` headers in spec.md        |\n636-| Phases       | `### Phase` headers in plan.md             |\n637-| Tasks        | `- [ ] T##` or `- [ ] TASK-` in tasks.md   |\n638-\n639-### Examples\n640-\n641-**Warning (under-scoped for Level 2):**\n642-```\n643-Declared Level: 2\n644-Found: 1 user story, 2 phases, 8 tasks\n645-Expected: 2-4 stories, 3-5 phases, 15-50 tasks\n646-```\n647-\n648-**Warning (over-scoped for Level 1):**\n--\n788:**Rule:** `SPEC_DOC_SUFFICIENCY`, diagnostics `SPECDOC_SUFFICIENCY_005` (budget) and `SPECDOC_SUFFICIENCY_006` (binding)  \n789-**Description:** A packet `goal.md` carries the directive an operator sets as the session objective. Every runtime goal surface caps what it holds and truncates at the tail, where the completion criteria live, so the slice that gets set has a budget.\n790-\n791-### What Is Measured\n792-\n793-The durable slice: everything after the frontmatter's closing `---` up to `<!-- ANCHOR:log -->`, anchors and comments included, counted in characters. The frontmatter is never part of it and never leaves the file. The log is volatile and never part of it.\n"}}],"_meta":{"cognition.ai/cwd":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w776a5f2639/tree","terminal_exit":{"terminal_id":"dc91a4","exit_code":0,"signal":null},"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
